course: dictionary
lesson: 2859
title: Dollars and cents
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A dollar is a unit of money used in countries such as the United States, Canada, Australia and New Zealand. One dollar is worth 100 cents, so cents are the small parts of a dollar. When you see the sign $, the number is an amount of dollars.
explain: Dollars are used for prices, savings, spending and earning. Spending takes money away, and earning adds money. «Paying 1 dollar for 2 apples at 45 cents each» shows this: the apples cost 90 cents, and 100 - 90 = 10 cents come back.
explain: Read the wording carefully. «A dollar an hour» means one dollar for each hour, and «5 dollars each» means every item costs 5 dollars. To find a total, multiply: 6 × 5 = 30 dollars.
fact: A dollar is 100 cents.
fact: The sign for dollars is $, as in $5.
fact: In Canada the one-dollar coin is called a loonie, because it has a loon on it, and the two-dollar coin is called a toonie.
fact: A dollar can be a coin or a note, and a note is often called a dollar bill.
fact: Dollars are used in the United States, Canada, Australia, New Zealand and some other countries.
exercise: You pay 1 dollar for 2 apples that cost 45 cents each. How much change comes back? | 45 cents | 90 cents | *10 cents | 55 cents
exercise: «3 kites cost 15 dollars.» How much does one kite cost? | 3 dollars | *5 dollars | 15 dollars | 12 dollars
exercise: What is the Canadian one-dollar coin called? | toonie | cent | dollar bill | *loonie
test: How many cents are in one dollar? | 50 | 1000 | *100 | 10
test: What does the sign $ tell you? | A length in metres | A time of day | *A number of dollars | A number of cents
exam: In «a boy spent over a thousand dollars in a week», what does spent show? | *Money was taken away by buying things | Money was found on the ground | Money was earned and added to the family's savings | Money was counted and put in a bank
